  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Business Enabling Project (BEP)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Zambia):  In Zambia, policy challenges across many sectors inhibit private sector growth and discourage foreign and domestic investment into local businesses. Market-distorting government interventions in agricultural markets, restrictions on international trade in commodities, and poorly functioning marketing systems deter agribusinesses. In the energy sector, there is a need to expand access to electricity for households and businesses, improve electricity reliability, and diversify electricity generation sources. The Government of Zambia (GRZ) seeks to create one million jobs by 2030 and increase the contribution of wildlife and forests to the country&apos;s economic growth through tourism. This goal is stymied by poor policy enforcement and illicit demands on natural resources such as poaching, charcoal production, and timber extraction. The Business Enabling Project (BEP) supports development and implementation of policies and regulations that catalyze private sector investment and trade by strengthening Zambian government and civil society organizations to improve and streamline policies and processes. BEP supports GRZ structures such as the new Public-Private Dialogue Forum and its sector working groups to enhance buy-in and sustainability of the private sector. If the GRZ creates a business-enabling environment that is informed with input from key stakeholders, then private sector companies will be supported to grow their businesses, expand trade and attract increased investment in agriculture, energy, as well as nature-based tourism and natural resource management that support biodiversity conservation. This is anticipated to result in broad-based gender-equitable economic growth that transforms rural areas and pulls people out of poverty.Under USAID&apos;s CDCS, the mechanism supports Development Objective 2: Reduce Rural Poverty through Enterprise-Driven Inclusive Economic Growth, andthe Administration&apos;s policy priority of private sector engagement..</narrative>
  </description>
